### Logical Relationships (Tree Structure)

```
G1 -> G2, G3, G4, G5, G6          [AND][;]
  G2 -> AT1, AT2                    [AND][-]
  G3 -> G3.1 OR G3.2                [OR][FALLBACK(G3.1,G3.2)]
    G3.1 -> AT4                      [AND][-]
    G3.2 -> AT5                      [AND][-]
  G4 -> G4.1, G4.2, G4.3, G4.4    [AND][;]
    G4.1 -> AT2                      [AND][-]
    G4.2 -> AT6                      [AND][-]
    G4.3 -> AT7                      [AND][-]
    G4.4 -> AT8                      [AND][-]
  G5 -> G5.1 OR G5.2 OR G5.3      [OR][FALLBACK(G5.1,G5.2,G5.3)]
    G5.1 -> AT9                      [AND][-]
    G5.2 -> AT10                     [AND][-]
    G5.3 -> AT11                     [AND][-]
  G6 -> G6.1 OR G6.2                [OR][FALLBACK(G6.1,G6.2)]
    G6.1 -> AT12                     [AND][-]
    G6.2 -> AT13                     [AND][-]
```

**Explanation of the chosen operators**

* **Sequential (`;`)** – Used when a clear order of operations is required (e.g., request handling → delivery → dish retrieval).  
* **Parallel (`#`)** – Not used in this model because all sub‑goals must be finished before moving to the next high‑level goal.  
* **Fallback (`FALLBACK(...)`)** – Employed for alternative delivery methods (table vs. tray), dish retrieval strategies, and door‑opening partners. The robot first tries the simplest/most reliable option; if it fails, it falls back to the next one.  

**AND/OR relations**

* **AND** – When all child goals/tasks must be achieved for the parent to succeed (e.g., G1’s sub‑goals).  
* **OR** – When any one of the alternatives is sufficient (e.g., G3’s delivery methods, G5’s dish‑retrieval strategies, G6’s door‑opening partners).  

This goal model captures all the mission requirements, uncertainties, and cooperation possibilities in a clear hierarchical structure suitable for multi‑robot planning.
